"""Netherlands flagship course content (10). Own wording."""
from flagship_nl_sg_au import M
def K(id, title, summary, task, cond, std, safety, outs, jobs, mods):
    return dict(id=id, title=title, summary=summary, task=task, cond=cond, std=std, safety=safety, outcomes=outs, jobs=jobs, mods=mods)
C = []
SAFE = "Learning only. Follow your local law and get written permission before you work with real customers, premises, data or money."
C.append(K('NL-01', 'Food Industry and Food Technology', 'Plan, make, price and sell one small food product safely and with low waste, from first idea to a 30-day trial with real buyers.',
 'Run a 30-day trial of one small food product with one local buyer and keep a full record.', 'About 150 hours over 3 to 6 months. Licensed shared kitchen or partner premises, scale, thermometer, free spreadsheet, a mentor and a peer circle.',
 'Hazard plan complete; every batch logged; cost sheet reviewed; label checked with the local food authority; at least 70% on the check.', "Process learning only. Sell only from premises and with registrations your local food authority accepts.",
 [('Understand','Explain how a hazard plan stops harm in a food process.'),('Apply','Scale a recipe and log yield, time and temperature.'),('Analyse','Find the biggest cost and the biggest waste in a batch.'),('Create','Design a pack, a label draft and a trial plan.')],
 ['Small-batch food maker','Production assistant','Quality record keeper','Food supply coordinator'],
 [M('Find a gap and a buyer','Understand',5,'Good food products start with a person who will pay, not with a recipe you like. In this module you look at what buyers already purchase and where they are unhappy. You finish with one named buyer and a product idea that fits a real gap.','List 10 products on sale near you and write the price per kilo and the kind of buyer for each one.|Look for gaps in taste, diet, pack size, freshness or price, because a gap is a reason for a buyer to switch.|Ask five buyers what they buy, how often they buy it and what annoys them, and write the answers down word for word.|Name one buyer who has said they would try a sample, since a named buyer is worth more than a guess about a crowd.|A common mistake is to ask friends, who will say they like everything, so ask people who pay for the product today.','Five cafes buy bought-in biscuits at EUR 0.60 each and 4 of 5 (80%) complain about stale stock. Each cafe buys 40 biscuits a week, so one cafe spends 40 x 0.60 = EUR 24 a week and all five spend 5 x 24 = EUR 120 a week. Winning 2 cafes would be worth 2 x 24 = EUR 48 a week.','Complete a market table of 10 products and five buyer notes, then write one sentence naming your product and your first buyer.','Price ten products per kilo, hear five buyers, and name one buyer with a weekly quantity.','Why talk to buyers before you work on the recipe?','Because a buyer who will pay defines the product, the price and the pack, so the recipe can be built to fit them instead of being guessed.'),
  M('Hazard thinking','Apply',6,'A hazard plan is a short list of what can go wrong at each step and what stops it. It is the main tool that keeps food safe for the people who eat it. You learn to draw your own process and test each step with the same questions.','Draw your process as boxes from buying to selling, and ask at each box what can go wrong.|Sort each hazard into one of four types: germs, chemical, foreign bodies and allergens.|Mark the control points where a step makes food safe, such as cooking, cooling, cold storage, cleaning and hand washing.|Set a limit for each control point, keep a daily log and write what you do when a limit fails.|A common mistake is to write a plan once and never use it, so the log must be filled in on every production day.','Step: cool cooked rice. Germs grow if the rice stays warm, so your plan sets a limit of 5 C or below within 3 hours (180 minutes). The log shows 14:00 start at 70 C, 15:30 reading 12 C and 16:45 reading 4 C. Total time is 2 h 45 min = 165 minutes, which is within the 180-minute limit, so the batch passes. Had it taken 200 minutes, the rule is to discard it.','Draw the flow of your product and mark every hazard, control, limit and corrective action on it.','Give every step a hazard question and a control, list allergens, and give each limit a number and an action if it fails.','Name the four hazard types and say what you do when a limit fails.','Biological (germs), chemical, physical (foreign bodies) and allergens. When a limit fails you follow the written action, such as discarding the batch, and record it.'),
  M('Recipe, scaling and yield','Apply',10,'A home recipe is not a production recipe, because production needs exact weights and a repeatable method. You will weigh everything, change one thing at a time and record what you get. The result is a final recipe you can scale with confidence.','Write every ingredient in grams, not cups or spoons, because volume changes with packing and humidity.|Remember that salt, spice, raising agent and bake time do not scale in a straight line, so test each larger batch.|Calculate yield as weight out divided by weight in, times 100, and write it on every batch record.|Change only one thing between trial batches so you know what caused each difference.|A common mistake is to scale the recipe by eye, so always weigh the dough before and the biscuits after baking.','A 1.0 kg dough gives 0.85 kg baked, so yield is 85%. At 5.0 kg you expect 5.0 x 0.85 = 4.25 kg, which is 4250 / 40 = about 106 biscuits of 40 g. If you get only 4.0 kg, yield is 4.0 / 5.0 = 80%, which is 5 points low and about 6 biscuits short, so you investigate the oven.','Build three trial batches, changing one thing in each, and record weight in, weight out and yield.','Complete three batch records with yield and write a final recipe with a reason for each change.','What is the yield when 4.0 kg comes out from 5.0 kg in?','4.0 / 5.0 = 0.80, so the yield is 80%.'),
  M('Shelf life and storage','Analyse',8,'Shelf life is what you can prove, not what you hope. In this module you keep samples in the pack you will sell and check them on set days. You then set a date label that is shorter than the tested life.','Pack samples in the final pack and store them at the temperature the buyer will use.|Check smell, look, texture and taste on days 1, 3, 7, 10 and 14, and record each result.|Take the last day with no fault as the tested life.|Set the label date shorter than the tested life to leave a safety margin, for example 70% of the tested life.|A common mistake is to test in an open tub, so test only in the sold pack.','Samples stored at 5 C pass on days 1, 3, 7 and 10 and fail on day 14. The tested life is 10 days. A 70% rule gives 10 x 0.70 = 7 days, so the label date is 7 days after making. The margin is 3 days, which is 30% of the tested life.','Run a shelf-life test with samples and set the label date from your records.','Record five dated sample records, and show that the label date is shorter than the tested life with a margin.','Tested life is 10 days and you keep a 30% margin. What date label do you set?','10 x 0.70 = 7 days.'),
  M('Packs and labels','Create',8,'A label is both a legal paper and a sales tool. It must give true information in a clear order. You draft the label and have an expert check it before you print.','Show name, ingredients in order of weight, allergens in bold, net weight, batch code, date and your contact, and ask your local food authority whether a nutrition table and which date wording (best before or use by) apply to your product.|Fill packs to at least the net weight on the label and check with a scale.|Print no health claims, because they can be false or illegal and cannot be proven by a small trial.|Ask your local food authority to check the draft label before printing a large number.|A common mistake is to print the label before checking the rules, so keep the first print small.','A 4.25 kg batch fills 4.25 / 0.25 = 17 packs of 250 g. Label text: Oat biscuits. Ingredients: oats, wheat flour, butter, sugar. Contains milk, wheat. 250 g. Batch 1705. Best before 24.05. A check of 5 packs on the scale shows 252, 251, 254, 250 and 253 g, so all are at least 250 g and the mean is 1260 / 5 = 252 g.','Draft the label, weigh five packs and get one expert comment.','Check that all items are present, allergens are highlighted, no claims are made, and all five packs are at or above net weight.','Why avoid health claims on labels?','They can be false or illegal and a small trial cannot prove them.'),
  M('Costing and pricing','Evaluate',12,'You must know the cost of one unit before you set a price. Many small makers forget labour, energy or waste and then lose money without noticing. In this module you build a cost sheet with a currency on every line.','Cost per unit = total cost divided by units made and sold.|Margin = (price - cost) divided by price, shown as a percentage.|Include ingredients, packs, energy, kitchen share, labour and waste on the sheet.|Ask your mentor to check the sheet for a missing cost, and calculate the margin twice.|A common mistake is to treat your own time as free, which makes the price too low to continue.','For 100 biscuits made and sold: ingredients EUR 14, packs 6, energy 3, kitchen 12 and labour 40 give 14 + 6 + 3 + 12 + 40 = EUR 75. Cost per biscuit is 75 / 100 = EUR 0.75. At a price of EUR 1.20, revenue is EUR 120, profit is EUR 45 and margin is 45 / 120 = 37.5%.','Build a cost sheet for one batch with the currency on every line and a price decision.','Calculate the margin twice with the same result, so that the mentor finds no missing cost.','What is the margin at price EUR 1.20 and cost EUR 0.75?','(1.20 - 0.75) / 1.20 = 0.375, so 37.5%.'),
  M('Run the 30-day trial','Apply',60,'The trial is small, real and recorded. You sell to a real buyer for 30 days and keep a daily log so that every decision can be checked later. You also answer every complaint with a change.','Log date, batch, units made, units sold, waste and complaints every production day.|Keep one sample of every batch until its date has passed.|Answer each complaint within a day and write the change you made.|Check core temperature or another control point on every batch, not only on some.|A common mistake is to fix problems without writing them down, so record the cause as well as the fix.','In week 2 a batch of 80 packs brings back 12 returns, which is 12 / 80 = 15%. The cause is under-baking. After you add a core-temperature rule, the next batch of 80 packs brings back 2 returns, which is 2 / 80 = 2.5%. Returns fell by 12.5 percentage points.','Run 30 days and keep the log, then write a one-page summary of what you changed.','Record 30 days of results, and answer every complaint with a dated change.','What goes in the daily log?','Date, batch, units made, units sold, waste and complaints.'),
  M('Decide and plan','Evaluate',41,'At the end of the trial you compare the plan with what happened and decide what to do next. A decision backed by numbers is easier to explain to a buyer, a mentor or a lender. You also give feedback to peers.','Make a before and after table of waste, cost per unit, returns and buyer count.|Write three lessons in plain words, each with a number as evidence.|Decide to scale, change or stop, and write the reason.|Present to a review room and give two peers written feedback.|A common mistake is to decide on a feeling, so every choice must point to a figure in the table.','In the first week 14 of 100 biscuits were wasted (14%) and in the last week 5 of 100 (5%), so waste fell by 9 points. Of 9 buyers, 6 bought again, which is 6 / 9 = 67%. The decision is to scale to two cafes.','Present to a review room and give two peers written feedback.','Support the decision with at least three numbers from the trial.','What are the three decisions after a trial?','Scale, change or stop.')]))

C.append(K('NL-02', 'Handmade Goods and Craft Business', 'Turn a craft skill into a small product line that sells online and in person, with honest costs and a story buyers believe.',
 'Design, make, photograph, price and sell a small range of handmade goods and record 30 days of sales.', 'About 120 hours over 3 to 5 months. Basic tools, a phone camera, a free shop page or marketplace listing, a mentor and peer circle.',
 'Three finished products; photos and descriptions; full cost sheet; 30-day sales log; at least 70% on the check.', SAFE,
 [('Understand','Explain how design, craft and a buyer fit together.'),('Create','Make a small range with consistent quality.'),('Analyse','Work out true cost per piece including time.'),('Evaluate','Judge which product to repeat or drop.')],
 ['Craft maker','Product designer assistant','Online shop owner','Market stall seller'],
 [M('Choose a craft and a buyer','Understand',6,'Start with who will buy and why, not with what you enjoy making. In this module you pick one craft you can repeat and describe the buyer for it. You finish with a one-page product idea and a price band.','Pick one craft you can make well and repeat at the same quality.|Describe the buyer by age, need and price level, in two or three lines.|Visit five sellers or listings and write down price, material and quality for each product.|Compare the prices, find the range and choose a band where your costs can still earn a profit.|A common mistake is to copy the cheapest seller, so decide on value first and price second.','A leather card-holder maker notes five prices: EUR 25, 28, 30, 32 and 35. The mean is (25 + 28 + 30 + 32 + 35) / 5 = 150 / 5 = EUR 30. Buyers want slim holders in three colours, so the price band is EUR 25 to 35 with a target of EUR 30.','Write a one-page product idea with the buyer, price band and three products.','Name the buyer, price band and three products; note five sellers.','Why describe the buyer first?','Because price, style and sales channel all follow from who the buyer is.'),
  M('Design and prototype','Create',14,'A prototype is a cheap first version that you make to learn. You test it with real people and change it before you make a range. A design log keeps each change and the reason for it.','Sketch three options and choose one with the help of two or three people.|Build a prototype from the real materials, not from scrap that behaves differently.|Test it with five people and ask them to use it, not only to look at it.|Write each change and its reason in a design log with a date.|A common mistake is to change many things at once, so change one thing and test again.','Prototype 1: 3 of 5 testers say the strap is too stiff, which is 60%. You switch to softer leather. In prototype 2 only 1 of 5 says it is stiff, which is 20%. The log records the change, the reason and a fall of 40 percentage points.','Build two prototypes and write a design log with at least two changes.','Design log with at least two dated changes and their reasons.','What does a design log record?','Each change and the reason for it.'),
  M('Materials and quality','Apply',12,'Quality is repeatable, not lucky. A short written standard lets you judge each piece the same way and shows you when a supplier or a method has changed. You inspect every piece against it.','Write a one-page standard for size, finish and fault limits, with numbers wherever you can.|Buy materials in batches and keep receipts so you can trace a fault to a batch.|Inspect each piece against the standard and record pass or fail with the reason.|Calculate the reject rate each week and act when it rises.|A common mistake is to sell second-rate pieces without telling the buyer, so mark them clearly or remove them.','A candle maker rejects any piece with a crack over 2 mm. Of 20 pieces, 3 are rejected, so the reject rate is 3 / 20 = 15%. After slower cooling, 1 of 20 is rejected, which is 5%. The rate fell by 10 percentage points.','Build five pieces and inspect each against your standard.','Record an inspection for each piece and calculate the reject rate.','Why write a quality standard?','So each piece is judged the same way, by you and by anyone who helps.'),
  M('True cost and price','Evaluate',12,'Most makers forget their time and sell at a loss without knowing it. In this module you add up every cost for one piece, including your time at a fair rate, and set a price from a target margin.','Cost = materials + packing + fees + time at a fair hourly rate.|Price = cost divided by (1 - target margin), for example divided by 0.6 for a 40% margin.|Check the price against the band you found from five sellers.|Test the price with five buyers and note who says it is too high.|A common mistake is to add margin as cost x 1.4, which gives a smaller margin than 40%, so use the division.','Material EUR 6, packing 1, fees 2 and time 1.5 h at EUR 12 (EUR 18) give 6 + 1 + 2 + 18 = EUR 27. Price at a 40% margin is 27 / 0.6 = EUR 45. Check: profit is 45 - 27 = EUR 18, and 18 / 45 = 40%.','Build the cost sheet for three products and set a price for each.','Time included; margin calculated twice with the same result.','What price gives a 40% margin on a cost of EUR 27?','27 / 0.6 = EUR 45.'),
  M('Photos, words and story','Create',10,'Online buyers cannot touch your work, so the photos and words must do that job. You learn a simple photo method and write a listing that answers questions before they are asked.','Use natural light, a plain background and three angles: front, side and in use.|Show size with a hand or a ruler in one photo.|Write size, material, care and who made it in the first lines of the description.|Tell one true story of the product in two or three sentences.|A common mistake is to use filters that change the colour, which leads to returns.','You compare two listings of the same pot. Listing A has one photo and 100 views with 2 sales, which is 2%. Listing B has three photos, a size and care note and 100 views with 5 sales, which is 5%. At EUR 30 each, B earns 5 x 30 = EUR 150 and A earns 2 x 30 = EUR 60.','Photograph and write listings for three products.','Take clear photos, give size and care, and tell a true story.','Why add size and care to a listing?','Fewer returns and fewer questions from buyers.'),
  M('Selling online and in person','Apply',20,'Using two channels lets you compare. An online listing reaches many people slowly, while a stall or friend sale gives fast feedback. You track each channel for two weeks and learn which suits the product.','Open one online shop page or listing and one market, stall or friend sale.|Track views, questions and sales for each channel on one sheet.|Reply to each message within one day.|Note which products sell where and what buyers ask.|A common mistake is to compare channels by total sales only, so also compare sales per visitor.','Online: 120 views and 4 sales, which is 4 / 120 = 3.3%. Stall: 90 visitors stopped and 18 bought, which is 18 / 90 = 20%. Together 22 sales at about EUR 30 each is 22 x 30 = EUR 660. The stall works better for gifts.','Run both channels for two weeks and keep the sheet.','Show both channels with views or visitors in the sales log.','Why use two channels?','To compare results and learn which channel suits the product.'),
  M('Packing, post and returns','Apply',8,'The parcel is part of the product. A good pack protects the item and shows care, and a clear postage and returns note stops disputes. You test with one parcel before you sell.','Pack to survive a one-metre drop, using double wrap for glass or ceramic.|Weigh the packed parcel and check the postage price before you list.|Show postage clearly at checkout or in the listing.|Write a short returns note with the number of days and who pays, and check the legal minimum return period for online sales with your local authority.|A common mistake is to ignore postage in the price, which can wipe out the margin.','A product costs EUR 27 to make and sells at EUR 45. If you pay postage of EUR 6.50 yourself, profit is 45 - 27 - 6.50 = EUR 11.50, and margin is 11.50 / 45 = 25.6% instead of 40%. Showing postage at checkout keeps the 40% margin.','Pack and send one test parcel and record the weight and postage.','Send the parcel so that it arrives undamaged, and show postage in the listing.','Why price postage before you list?','So it does not wipe out the margin.'),
  M('Review and grow','Evaluate',38,'At the end you decide which products to keep, change or drop, using numbers from your own sales. You also ask buyers for feedback and plan the next batch.','Compare sales, margin and effort for each product.|Calculate profit per product as profit per piece times pieces sold.|Ask three buyers what they liked and what they would change.|Plan the next batch with quantities that match recent sales.|A common mistake is to keep a product because you like it, so let the numbers lead.','Product A: price EUR 45, margin 42%, so profit per piece is 45 x 0.42 = EUR 18.90, and 12 sold gives 12 x 18.90 = EUR 226.80. Product B: price EUR 20, margin 15%, so profit per piece is EUR 3.00 and 2 sold gives EUR 6.00. Drop B and repeat A.','Present results and a plan for the next batch.','Decide on each product with numbers.','How do you decide to drop a product?','When margin and sales are both low compared with the effort it takes.')]))

C.append(K('NL-03', 'Online Sales and Digital Commerce', 'Set up a small online shop, bring in visitors, take orders safely and measure what works.',
 'Launch a small online shop and run a 30-day sales trial with a daily record.', 'About 130 hours over 3 to 5 months. Computer, free shop tools, a small product range, a mentor and peer circle.',
 'Shop live; policies written; 30-day record of visits, orders and costs; report with three changes; at least 70% on the check.', SAFE + " Show clear prices, delivery and returns, and ask consent before storing buyer data.",
 [('Understand','Explain the steps from a visit to a delivered order.'),('Apply','Build and test the shop and a payment path.'),('Analyse','Read visits, orders and costs to find what works.'),('Evaluate','Choose which channel to keep.')],
 ['Online shop assistant','E-commerce coordinator','Digital seller','Customer service agent'],
 [M('The path from visit to order','Understand',6,'Every online sale passes the same steps, and each step can lose a buyer. If you can see the steps, you can find the weakest one and fix it first. You draw your own path with first guesses and later replace them with real counts.','Name the six steps: find, view, add to basket, pay, receive, review.|Write the number of people at each step so you can see where they drop away.|Find the biggest drop between two steps and mark it as the weak step.|Fix only the weakest step first, then measure again.|A common mistake is to work on getting more visitors when the page loses most of them at payment.','Of 100 visits, 40 add to basket (40%) and 4 pay. Of the 40 baskets, 36 leave, which is 36 / 40 = 90%. Overall conversion is 4 / 100 = 4%. The biggest drop is at payment (90% lost, against 60% lost before the basket), so you fix the payment step first.','Draw your path with guessed numbers at each step.','Draw six steps with numbers and mark the weak step.','Name the six steps.','Find, view, add to basket, pay, receive, review.'),
  M('Choose products and suppliers','Evaluate',10,'Pick items that you can deliver on time and still earn a margin after all fees. Samples and a comparison table keep you from promising what a supplier cannot do.','Check margin after payment fees and any other fees.|Order samples from two suppliers and test them yourself.|Record price, lead time and quality in one table.|Choose the supplier whose lead time you can honestly promise.|A common mistake is to pick the cheapest price and then wait weeks, so weigh lead time as much as cost.','Sell price is EUR 15 and the payment fee is EUR 0.60. Supplier A costs EUR 8 in 3 days: margin is 15 - 0.60 - 8 = EUR 6.40, which is 6.40 / 15 = 42.7%. Supplier B costs EUR 6 in 14 days: margin is EUR 8.40, which is 56%. You choose A for the trial because 14 days is too long to promise.','Compare two suppliers in a table with samples.','Build a table with price, lead time, quality and margin after fees.','Why test samples?','To check quality and delivery time before you promise buyers.'),
  M('Build the shop','Create',16,'A simple, clear shop beats a clever one. You build three product pages that show what the buyer needs: photos, size, price, stock, delivery and returns. You test them on a phone first.','Show photos, size, price and stock on each product page.|Make delivery time and returns rules visible before checkout.|Test every page on a phone and on a computer.|Ask five people to find the delivery information and time them.|A common mistake is to hide the delivery cost until the last step, which makes buyers leave.','Of 100 visits, 70 come from phones, so 70%. In a test, 4 of 5 people find the delivery note in under 10 seconds, which is 80%. The fifth cannot find it, so you move the delivery line above the buy button and test again.','Build three product pages and test them on two devices.','Complete the pages and pass the phone test with at least four of five people.','Why test on a phone first?','Most visitors use phones.'),
  M('Payments and rules','Apply',10,'Money and personal data need care. You use a licensed payment partner, show the full price clearly and publish the policies that buyers expect. You place test orders before you open.','Use a licensed payment partner and never store card numbers yourself.|Write short privacy, delivery and returns pages in plain words, and check the current consumer rules for distance sales, such as the cooling-off period, with your local authority.|Show the total price including tax and delivery before the buyer pays.|Ask consent before you store an email address, and say what you will use it for.|A common mistake is to add delivery only at the last step, so show it in the basket.','The item costs EUR 29 and delivery is EUR 6, so the total is 29 + 6 = EUR 35, tax included. If tax is 21% and included in the EUR 29, the tax part is 29 - 29 / 1.21 = EUR 5.03. Use the rate that applies where you sell.','Place three test orders and check the total, the policies and the confirmation.','Test orders complete; policies live; totals correct.','Why never store card numbers?','It is a high risk and is handled by the licensed payment partner.'),
  M('Bring visitors','Apply',20,'Without visitors there are no sales. You choose two free channels, publish useful content on a steady plan and track where each visit comes from. Tracking tells you which channel to keep.','Pick two free channels such as search, social posts or an email list.|Write one useful post a week that answers a buyer question.|Use a different link for each channel so the source of each visit is known.|Record visits and orders per channel every week.|A common mistake is to post only about the product, so give how-to help that people want to read.','A how-to post brings 40 visits and 3 orders, which is 3 / 40 = 7.5%. A social picture brings 100 visits and 2 orders, which is 2%. The how-to post sends fewer visitors but they buy more often, so you write more of them.','Run two weeks of posts with a link for each channel.','Track visits and orders per channel.','How do you know which channel works?','Track the source of each visit and each order.'),
  M('Run the 30 days','Apply',36,'During the trial you record every day and fix the weakest step every week. A steady habit shows what really changes results.','Log visits, orders, costs and questions every day.|Answer each message within one day.|Pick the weakest step each week and make one change.|Write each change with the date so you can see its effect.|A common mistake is to change many things in one week, so you cannot tell what worked.','In week 2 you shorten the payment page. Weekly orders rise from 4 to 9, which is an increase of 5, or 5 / 4 = 125%. By day 30 the log shows 900 visits and 27 orders, so conversion is 27 / 900 = 3%.','Run 30 days with a daily log.','Log complete; at least three changes made and dated.','What is logged daily?','Visits, orders, costs and questions.'),
  M('Read the numbers','Analyse',16,'Numbers show where to act. Three simple measures are enough: conversion, cost per order and repeat buyers. You build a one-page report that someone else can follow.','Conversion = orders divided by visits, as a percentage.|Cost per order = spend divided by orders.|Repeat rate = buyers who order again divided by all buyers.|Put the three measures on one page with a short comment under each.|A common mistake is to report totals only, so always show the rate as well.','With 200 visits and 8 orders, conversion is 8 / 200 = 4%. With EUR 40 spent, cost per order is 40 / 8 = EUR 5. If 2 of the 8 buyers order again, the repeat rate is 2 / 8 = 25%.','Build a one-page report with the three measures.','Calculate three measures correctly and comment on each.','What is conversion for 200 visits and 8 orders?','8 / 200 = 4%.'),
  M('Decide and plan','Evaluate',16,'You keep what works and drop what does not. Comparing channels by cost per order gives a fair basis for the choice, and a short plan sets the next 30 days.','Compare channels by cost per order, not by visits.|Choose one channel to grow and one to test.|Write three goals for the next 30 days with numbers.|Present the results and the plan to your review room.|A common mistake is to grow a channel with many visits but few orders.','Social spent EUR 90 for 10 orders, so cost per order is EUR 9. Email spent EUR 20 for 10 orders, so cost per order is EUR 2. Together 110 / 20 = EUR 5.50 per order. You grow email.','Present results and a 30-day plan.','Justify the decision with numbers.','Which channel do you grow?','The one with the lowest cost per order and steady quality.')]))

def S(id, title, summary, task, cond, std, outs, jobs, mods, safety=SAFE):
    C.append(K(id, title, summary, task, cond, std, safety, outs, jobs, mods))
O4 = lambda a,b,c,d: [('Understand',a),('Apply',b),('Analyse',c),('Create',d)]
S('NL-04','Mobile Applications','Design, build and test a small mobile app for one real user need, then measure how people use it.',
 'Build and test a small working app for one real user group and report 14 days of use.','About 140 hours over 3 to 5 months. Computer, free app-building tools, a phone for testing, five testers, a mentor and peer circle.',
 'Working app; user need statement; test notes from five users; privacy note; 14-day use report; at least 70% on the check.',
 [('Understand', 'Explain the app cycle from need to release.'), ('Apply', 'Build screens and a working flow.'), ('Analyse', 'Read test and use data to find faults.'), ('Create', 'Design a clear, accessible app screen set.')],
 ['App tester', 'Junior app builder', 'Product assistant', 'Support analyst'],
 [M('Find the need','Understand',8,'An app is worth building only when one group of people has one repeated problem that a simple tool cannot solve. This module teaches you to find that problem by listening, not by guessing. You finish with a single written need statement that guides every later choice.','Interview five people from your chosen group for 15 minutes each, and ask about the last time the problem happened rather than about ideas for an app.|Write the need as one sentence in the form: user, need, reason, for example a parent needs to log feeds because two adults share the task.|Count how many of the five people had the problem in the last week, since fewer than three out of five means the need is weak.|Check whether a shared note, a spreadsheet or a message group already solves it, and only continue if the app adds something clearly better.|A common mistake is to ask people if they would use an app, because people say yes to be polite; ask what they do today instead.','You interview 5 parents of toddlers and 4 say they lose track of feed times, which is 4 / 5 = 80%. A shared note was tried by 3 of them but became messy, so the need statement reads: a parent needs to log feeds quickly because two adults share the task.','Interview five people from your target group, record each answer in a table, and write one need statement of no more than 25 words.','Complete a table of five dated interviews, write a need statement that names a user, a need and a reason, and show that at least three of five people share the problem.','What three parts does a good need statement contain and why ask about the last time the problem happened?','It contains the user, the need and the reason. Asking about the last real event gives true behaviour, while asking about future app ideas gives polite guesses.'),
  M('Sketch and flow','Create',14,'Changing a paper sketch costs minutes, while changing finished code costs days. In this module you draw the screens and the path through them before you build anything. You then test the paper with real people to find confusion early.','Sketch five to eight screens on paper, one per card, and draw only boxes, labels and buttons so that nobody argues about colours.|Draw one arrow path for the main task and count the taps, aiming for three taps or fewer from opening the app to finishing the task.|Ask three people to use the paper by pointing at buttons, and swap the cards by hand when they tap, without explaining anything.|Write down every place a tester hesitates for more than three seconds, because each hesitation marks a fault in the design.|A common mistake is to add extra screens such as settings and profiles before the main task works, so cut every screen that the main task does not need.','Your feed log path is open the app, tap the feed type, tap save, which is 3 taps. Three testers finish in 12, 9 and 15 seconds, so the average is (12 + 9 + 15) / 3 = 12 seconds, and one tester hesitated at the save button.','Draw five to eight paper screens and the main task path, test them with three people, and write a list of every hesitation you saw.','Complete the main task in three taps or fewer, have three people test it, and list each hesitation with the screen where it happened.','Why should you count taps and what is a sign of a design fault during a paper test?','Counting taps shows how much effort the main task needs, and fewer taps is easier. A hesitation of more than a few seconds or a wrong tap shows the screen is unclear.'),
  M('Build the first version','Apply',30,'The first version should do one job and run on a real phone. Building small keeps you from getting lost and gives you something to test early. You will work in short versions and note what changed each time.','Choose a free app builder or a simple code framework that you can finish with, and avoid learning a second tool halfway through.|Build only the main task from your flow, for example save an entry and show a list of entries, and write every other idea on a later list.|Run the app on a real phone after every small change, because an emulator on a computer hides problems with touch and screen size.|Give each working state a version number such as 0.1, 0.2 and 0.3, and write one line saying what changed and what broke.|A common mistake is to build for weeks without testing, so set a rule that the app must run on the phone at least once every three working hours.','You plan 3 small builds: version 0.1 shows the screen layout (6 hours), version 0.2 saves an entry (10 hours) and version 0.3 shows the daily list (8 hours). That is 6 + 10 + 8 = 24 hours, which leaves 6 hours of a 30-hour module for phone testing and fixes.','Build and run the main task on a phone, and keep a version note with a date, a version number and one line of change for each build.','Run the main task from start to finish on a real phone, and write at least three version notes that show what changed.','Why should version 1 contain only the main task and why run it on a real phone?','A small version can be finished and tested quickly, so faults appear early. A real phone shows true touch, speed and screen problems that a computer hides.'),
  M('Data, privacy and safety','Apply',14,'Any app that stores information about people carries a duty to protect it. The safest data is data you never collect. In this module you decide what to store, write it in plain words and plan how a user can have it deleted.','List every piece of data the app stores and cross out each item that the main task does not need, such as full name, birth date or location.|Write a one-page note in plain words saying what is stored, why, where it is kept, who can see it and how a user asks for deletion.|Never store passwords as plain text and prefer a sign-in method that a trusted service handles, because a leak of readable passwords harms users on other sites too.|Ask for permission on the phone only at the moment the feature needs it, and make the app still work if the user says no.|Check with your local authority which data-protection rules apply, and have an adviser read your note before you share the app with outside testers.','Your feed log keeps 3 fields: time, feed type and amount, and no names. With 5 testers logging 6 feeds a day for 14 days you hold 5 x 6 x 14 = 420 entries, none of which identify a person, so a leak would cause very little harm.','Write a one-page privacy note that lists each data field, the reason for it, where it is stored and how a user can delete it.','Write a note that lists every field with a reason, names a deletion route, and contains no data that the main task does not need.','Why is collecting less data a safer choice, and what must the privacy note tell a user?','Data you never collect cannot leak or be misused. The note must say what is stored, why, where, who can see it and how to delete it.'),
  M('Design for everyone','Create',14,'People use apps in bright sun, with tired eyes, with one hand, or with a screen reader. Accessible design makes the app easier for all of them. You will check your screens against a short list and fix what fails.','Use text at least 16 points in size and test that every screen is readable without zooming in.|Keep a colour contrast ratio of at least 4.5 to 1 between normal text and its background, and check it with a free contrast checker.|Make every tap target at least 44 by 44 points, about 7 millimetres, so a thumb can hit it without touching its neighbour.|Never show an error or status with colour alone, and add words or a symbol, because about 1 in 12 men cannot tell some colours apart.|Give every button and image a text label that a screen reader can speak, and test the main task once with the screen reader turned on.','Your save button measures 30 x 30 points, which is smaller than 44 x 44, so you enlarge it to 48 x 48 points. Your error message was red only, so you add the words Save failed and a warning symbol next to it.','Check every screen against a six-item list covering text size, contrast, tap size, colour use, labels and screen reader, and fix each failed item.','Test all screens against the six checks so that each passes or has a dated fix, and complete the main task once with a screen reader.','Why must tap targets be large, and why is colour alone not enough to show an error?','Large targets stop mistaken taps for people with big thumbs, shaky hands or one-handed use. Some users cannot tell colours apart, so words or symbols must carry the message too.'),
  M('Test with users','Analyse',20,'Watching people use your app shows faults that you can no longer see yourself. The skill is to give a task, stay quiet and write down what happens. You then fix the faults that affect the most people.',"Recruit five testers who match your user group, because five testers usually reveal most of the biggest faults.|Give each tester one task in a sentence, such as log a feed and find today's list, and do not explain how or point at anything.|Write down the screen, the time and the words of the tester each time they stop, tap the wrong thing or ask a question.|Count how many testers hit each fault and sort the list so that faults hitting three or more testers are fixed first.|Fix the top three faults, then test again with the same task to prove the fix worked, rather than assuming it did.",'Four of five testers miss the save button, which is 4 / 5 = 80%. You move it to the bottom and enlarge it, and in a second round only 1 of 5 misses it, which is 20%, so the fix cut the failure rate by 60 percentage points.','Test the app with five users on one task, produce a fault log with counts, fix the top three faults and re-test them.','List each fault in the fault log with a count out of five, fix the three most common faults, and re-test to show fewer testers hitting them.','Why stay quiet during a user test and how do you decide which faults to fix first?','Staying quiet shows real confusion instead of guided success. Fix first the faults that the most testers hit, especially those that block the main task.'),
  M('Release and measure','Evaluate',30,'A small release to a trusted group tells you whether people really use the app. Numbers such as daily users and task completions are more honest than opinions. You will collect a few of them for 14 days.','Share the app with a small trusted group of 10 to 15 people and tell them it is a trial, so they expect rough edges.|Decide before release which three numbers you will count, for example daily active users, share of people who return after a week, and share of attempts that finish the main task.|Count from a simple log or a spreadsheet each day, and write the date, the number of users and the number of completed tasks.|Ask each user one open question each week, such as what almost made you stop, and record the answer in their own words.|A common mistake is to count downloads or installs only, which says nothing about use, so always count returns and completions.','After 14 days, 12 people installed the app and 9 opened it again on day 7, so return rate is 9 / 12 = 75%. Users tried the main task 120 times and finished it 84 times, so completion is 84 / 120 = 70%.','Run a 14-day release to a small group, keep a daily count of the three chosen numbers, and write one page of what the numbers show.','Record all 14 days, calculate the three numbers correctly, and write a page that names one thing the numbers prove and one they do not.','Why count returns and completions instead of installs alone?','An install only shows curiosity. Returns show the app was useful enough to open again, and completions show people can finish the main task.'),
  M('Decide and plan','Evaluate',10,'After a trial you must decide whether to improve, pause or stop. A fair decision uses numbers and compares impact with effort. You will rank the faults and write a short plan with dates.','List every fault and request from the trial, and give each an impact score from 1 to 3 times the number of users it affected.|Estimate the effort for each item in days, and divide impact by effort to get a value per day of work.|Choose the top two or three items that fit the time you really have, and leave the rest on a later list instead of promising everything.|Write three lessons in plain words, one about the user need, one about the build and one about the testing.|Set a review date and one number that will tell you whether the next version worked.','Fault A hit 8 users with severity 3, so impact is 8 x 3 = 24, and costs 2 days, giving 24 / 2 = 12 per day. Fault B hit 3 users with severity 2, so impact is 6, costs 1 day, giving 6 per day, so you fix A first.','Rank at least five faults by impact divided by effort, then write a next-version plan with dates, one target number and three lessons.','Show the sums in the ranking, write a plan with dates and a target number, and point each of the three lessons to evidence from the trial.','How do you rank fixes, and why divide impact by effort?','Multiply severity by the users affected to get impact, then divide by effort in days. This favours fixes that help many people for little work.')],
 safety=SAFE)
S('NL-05','IT Business and Managed Services','Set up a small IT service for a local client, deliver it for 30 days and report what you did and found.',
 'Deliver a managed IT service to one small client for 30 days and report results.','About 150 hours over 3 to 6 months. Computer, free tools for tickets and monitoring, a friendly client, mentor and peer circle.',
 'Service catalogue; onboarding checklist; 30-day ticket log; monthly report; security checks; at least 70% on the check.',
 [('Understand', 'Explain managed service against fix-when-broken.'), ('Apply', 'Onboard a client and run daily service.'), ('Analyse', 'Report tickets, risks and cost.'), ('Create', 'Design a service catalogue.')],
 ['IT support technician', 'Service desk analyst', 'Small IT business owner', 'Systems assistant'],
 [M('What managed means','Understand',8,"A managed service looks after a client's IT every month for an agreed fee, instead of waiting for something to break. This module explains the difference clearly so you can describe your service to a non-technical owner. You also learn why steady cost and fast response are what clients buy.","In a managed service you check updates, backups, security and devices on a schedule, so most problems are prevented before the client notices.|In a break-fix service you act only when called, bill by the hour, and the client carries all the risk of surprise bills and long outages.|Promise response times in writing, for example urgent within 1 hour and normal within 1 working day, because clients judge you by speed and clarity.|Explain value in the client's words, such as fewer lost working hours, rather than in technical words such as patch cycles.|A common mistake is to promise that nothing will ever fail, so promise instead how fast you respond and how you protect data.",'A 10-person office has 3 call-outs in a month, each taking 2 hours at EUR 70 an hour, so the break-fix bill is 3 x 2 x 70 = EUR 420, and next month it could be EUR 0 or EUR 900. A managed plan costs a fixed EUR 400 and includes help desk, updates and backup checks.','Write a half-page description of your service in plain words, with one example of a problem you would prevent and one you would respond to.','Check that a non-technical reader can say back what is included, how fast you respond and what the monthly cost covers.','How does a managed service differ from break-fix in cost, risk and timing?','Managed work is preventive, planned and billed at a steady fee, so the client has no surprise costs. Break-fix is reactive, billed by the hour and leaves the client carrying the risk.'),
  M('Catalogue and service levels','Create',14,'A catalogue says exactly what you do, how fast and for how much. Without it, clients assume everything is included and you lose money. In this module you write two or three clear levels and the exclusions that protect you.','Offer two or three levels, such as Basic and Standard, and make each one a clear step up so that a client can choose in a minute.|List inclusions as plain tasks, for example help desk during working hours, monthly updates, weekly backup check and a yearly device review.|Set response targets by priority, such as urgent 1 hour, normal 1 working day and request 3 working days, and state the hours when the clock runs.|List exclusions in writing, such as new hardware purchases, building work, and problems caused by software you did not install.|Show the price per user per month and the minimum number of users, so a client can calculate the monthly total alone.','Basic costs EUR 25 per user a month with help desk and updates, and Standard costs EUR 40 with backup checks and a yearly review. For a 10-person office that is 10 x 25 = EUR 250 for Basic or 10 x 40 = EUR 400 for Standard each month.','Write a one-page catalogue with two levels, response targets, exclusions and a price per user, and test it by asking a stranger to explain it back.','List inclusions, exclusions, response targets and prices in currency in the catalogue, and check that the stranger can explain the difference between levels.','Why list exclusions, and what three things must each service level state?','Exclusions prevent arguments about scope. Each level must state what is included, how fast you respond and what it costs.'),
  M('Price and break-even','Evaluate',16,'A good price pays for your time, tools, risk and a profit. Break-even tells you how many clients you need before the business covers its costs. You will build a sheet and test the price with real prospects.','List your fixed monthly costs, such as tools, insurance, phone, training and a fair wage for your time, with the currency on every line.|Work out the margin per client as the monthly fee minus the direct cost of serving that client, such as licences and your hours.|Break-even clients equal fixed costs divided by margin per client, and always round up to a whole client.|Add a one-off onboarding charge or count the extra hours, because the first month takes far more work than later months.|Show your price to two prospects and note their reaction, since a price no one questions is probably too low.','Your fixed costs are EUR 900 a month and each client gives a margin of EUR 260, so break-even is 900 / 260 = 3.46, which rounds up to 4 clients. With 6 clients the margin is 6 x 260 = EUR 1560, and the profit is 1560 - 900 = EUR 660.','Build a break-even sheet with fixed costs, margin per client and break-even clients, and show one profit line for 6 clients.','Put a currency on every line, round the break-even up correctly, and include in the sheet a profit figure for a chosen number of clients.','What is the break-even number of clients for fixed costs of EUR 900 and margin of EUR 260 per client, and why round up?','900 / 260 = 3.46, so you need 4 clients. You round up because you cannot sign part of a client and 3 clients would not cover the costs.'),
  M('Agreement and permission','Apply',14,"You may only touch a client's systems with written permission. A short, clear agreement protects both sides and sets the rules for data and exit. In this module you draft a one-page agreement and have a qualified adviser review it.","Write the scope in plain words: which devices, accounts and services you will manage and which you will not.|State the price, the billing date, the term and the notice period, for example a monthly term with 30 days' notice by either side.|Say who owns the data, how you handle passwords, and what happens to the client's data and access when the agreement ends.|Keep a change record that lists the date, what you changed and who approved it, so any problem can be traced.|Have a qualified adviser read any template before use and check with your local authority which rules apply to data and contracts.","Your one-page agreement lists 12 devices, a fee of EUR 450 a month, a 3-month start term, and 30 days' notice. The adviser's comments add a line that you will not store the client's passwords in plain text.","Draft a one-page agreement covering scope, price, term, notice, data and exit, and record the adviser's comments and your changes.",'Include all six parts in the agreement, obtain comments from a qualified adviser, and record each comment with the change you made.','Why do you need written permission, and what should happen to data when an agreement ends?',"Written permission shows the client agreed to your access and protects both sides. At the end, access is removed and the client's data is returned or deleted as agreed."),
  M('Onboard the client','Apply',30,'The first weeks decide whether a client trusts you. Onboarding builds an inventory and fixes the basics that protect the client straight away. You work from a checklist so that nothing is skipped.','List every device, account, licence and key service in a table, with the owner, age and condition of each item.|Check the basics in this order: working backup, updates switched on, strong unique passwords with a second step at sign-in, and a simple antivirus or protection tool.|Photograph or note the network layout, and record where the router and key switches are.|Give the client a one-page help guide saying how to ask for help, what counts as urgent and what to do while waiting.|A common mistake is to start changing things before a backup exists, so make a tested backup first.','The inventory finds 12 devices, of which 3 laptops are over 5 years old, and no backup exists. At 20 minutes per device the inventory takes 12 x 20 = 240 minutes, which is 4 hours, and you set up backup before any update.','Onboard one client with a written checklist, an inventory table, working basics and a one-page help guide.','Tick the checklist fully with dates, cover every device in the inventory, and test a backup before any other change.','What comes first in onboarding and why?','Inventory and a tested backup come first. Without a backup, any later change or update can cause permanent data loss.'),
  M('Run 30 days','Apply',40,'Managed service is won in daily habits. You log every ticket, apply updates in a planned window and prove that backups can be restored. At the end of the month you have honest data to report.','Log every ticket with the time received, the time first answered, the time solved, the priority and the cause.|Answer within the promised target and tell the client when a fix will take longer than expected.|Apply updates in a planned window outside working hours and tell the client the day before.|Test restoring one file and one full folder from backup during the month, and note how long the restore took.|Keep a short weekly note of what repeated, since repeating faults point to a root cause that you should fix once.','In week 2 there were 14 tickets and 12 were answered within target, so 12 / 14 = 86% were on target. The restore test brought back a 2 GB folder in 25 minutes, which you note as proof that the backup works.','Run the service for 30 days with a ticket log, a weekly note and one proven backup restore.','List all tickets with times in the log, record the restore test with its duration, and name any repeating problem in the weekly notes.','Why test a restore and what do you record for every ticket?','A backup that cannot be restored gives false safety, so a test proves it works. Each ticket records times received, answered and solved, plus priority and cause.'),
  M('Report','Analyse',20,'A monthly report shows the client what they received and what to do next. Clear numbers build trust and make renewal easy. You will write a one-page report in plain language.',"Show the number of tickets, the share answered within target and the longest outage in hours.|List the top three repeating problems with a cause and a proposed fix for each.|Add a short risk list, such as devices out of support, and the cost of fixing each risk in currency.|End the report with next month's actions, each with an owner and a date.|Keep the report to one page and write it for the owner, not for another technician.",'You handled 22 tickets and 20 were within target, so 20 / 22 = 91%. One printer caused 5 tickets, so you recommend replacing it for EUR 180 instead of paying about 5 x 20 minutes of repeat help each month.','Write and present a one-page monthly report with ticket numbers, target rate, top three problems, risks and next actions.',"Match the report's numbers to the ticket log, give each risk a cost, and give every action an owner and a date.",'What should a monthly report show and who is it written for?','It shows ticket numbers, response against target, repeating problems, risks and next actions. It is written for the client owner in plain words.'),
  M('Grow','Evaluate',8,'Growth is only good if quality stays high. You find how many clients you can serve with your hours and decide what to automate or stop. A short plan with numbers guides the next quarter.','Measure your hours per client per week from the ticket log, including onboarding and reports.|Divide your productive hours per week, normally about 30 of a 40-hour week, by the hours per client to get capacity.|Choose one tool or template that saves time, such as a standard onboarding checklist, and estimate the hours saved per client.|Decide whether to grow, change prices or stop a service, and write the reason in one sentence with a number.|Set a rule that you stop taking clients when response targets fall below 90 percent.','Each client needs 6 hours a week, and you have 30 productive hours, so capacity is 30 / 6 = 5 clients. A template that saves 1 hour per client cuts the need to 5 hours, so capacity rises to 30 / 5 = 6 clients.','Present a growth plan with your hours per client, your capacity, one time-saving change and a stop rule.','Show the capacity calculation in the plan, measure one saving in hours, and set a stop rule with a number.','How do you find how many clients you can serve?','Divide your productive weekly hours by the hours each client needs per week, for example 30 / 6 = 5 clients.')],
 safety=SAFE)
S('NL-06','Childcare Products and Services','Plan a safe, small childcare product or service for families, test it with parents and record the results.',
 'Design and trial one childcare product or service for 30 days with real families and record feedback and safety checks.','About 124 hours over 3 to 5 months. Partner setting or families who consent, free forms, a qualified adviser, mentor and peer circle.',
 'Safety plan reviewed by a qualified adviser; consent forms; 30-day record; parent feedback; at least 70% on the check.',
 [('Understand', 'Explain safety first in childcare.'), ('Apply', 'Run a small trial with consent.'), ('Analyse', 'Analyse parent feedback and incidents.'), ('Create', 'Design a product or service sheet.')],
 ['Childcare assistant', 'Family service coordinator', 'Baby product seller', 'Parent-support helper'],
 [M('Know the need','Understand',8,'Parents choose a childcare product or service because they trust it and it makes their day easier. This module teaches you to ask parents about real problems and to choose one need. You will not design anything until you hear the same problem more than once.','Interview ten parents for 10 to 15 minutes each, and ask what was hardest last week, not what they would like you to make.|Write each problem on a card and count how many parents mention it, then rank the cards from most to least frequent.|Ask what they do now and what it costs in time or money, so you can see whether your idea would be better.|Choose one need that at least four of ten parents share and that you can serve safely with your skills.|A common mistake is to pick a need because you like the idea, so let the count decide instead.','Seven of ten parents, which is 70%, say last-minute school pick-ups are hard. The other three split between sleep and meals, so you choose pick-up help as the need and write it as: working parents need a safe pick-up helper because they cannot leave work early.','Interview ten parents, count the problems, and write a one-sentence need and user description.','Record ten interviews, count the problems, and choose a need shared by at least four parents.','Why ask parents what was hardest instead of what they want?','Past problems are real facts, while wishes are guesses. Real problems show which service parents would actually use and pay for.'),
  M('Safety and law first','Apply',16,'Children must be safe before any product or service is designed. Rules on ages, ratios, clearance and product testing differ by place. In this module you write a safety plan and have a qualified adviser review it.','Check with your local authority which rules apply to your product or service, including age limits, adult-to-child ratios and any required clearance.|List every hazard, such as choking, falls, burns, allergies and getting lost, and write the control for each one.|For a product, test small parts with the small-parts test cylinder, which is about 3 centimetres wide, and treat any part that fits inside as a choking risk for children under 3.|Never be alone with a child unless you have the clearance that the rules require.|Ask a qualified adviser to read the plan, write their comments in the plan and change it before any trial.','If a local rule allows 1 adult for up to 8 children aged 6 to 10, then 12 children need 12 / 8 = 1.5, so 2 adults. Your toy for ages 2 and over has a bead that fits in the test cylinder, so you replace it with a larger bead before the trial.',"Write a safety plan with hazards, controls, age limits and rules checked, and get a qualified adviser's written comments.","Give each hazard a control, name the rules checked with the date, and record the adviser's comments in the plan.",'What must be done before you design or trial anything with children?','Check the safety rules with your local authority, write a hazard plan and have a qualified adviser review it.'),
  M('Consent and data','Apply',10,'Families trust you with information about their children. You must ask permission first and collect only what you need. This module shows you how to write a clear consent form and keep data safe.','Write a consent form in plain words that says what you will do, when, who will be present and how to reach you.|Collect the minimum child data, such as first name, age and allergies, and never collect photos, addresses or school names unless essential.|Say how long you keep the data, for example delete within 30 days after the trial, and how a parent can ask for deletion.|Store paper forms in a locked place and digital forms in a protected file with a password.|Tell parents they can withdraw at any time without giving a reason, and stop at once if they do.','Your form asks 4 things only: first name, age, allergies and an emergency phone number. For 6 families that is 6 x 4 = 24 data items, kept in one locked folder and deleted 30 days after the trial.','Draft a one-page consent form and a data note, and ask one parent to explain it back to you in their own words.','Write a form that covers purpose, data, storage time, deletion and withdrawal, and check that the parent explains it correctly.','Why collect the minimum child data, and what must a consent form say?','Less data means less harm if it leaks. The form must state what you will do, what data you hold, how long, how to delete it and that parents may withdraw.'),
  M('Design the offer','Create',16,'A clear offer tells families what you do, for whom, when and what you will not do. Honest limits protect children and build trust. You produce a one-page sheet that a parent can read in two minutes.','State what the product or service is in one sentence, with the age range and the times or places it is available.|Say what is included and, just as clearly, what is not, such as medicine, swimming or transport by car.|Describe the safety steps in simple words, for example two adults present and an allergy check on arrival.|Give the price per visit or per item and how to pay, and avoid claims you cannot prove such as the safest in town.|Test the sheet with two parents and change every sentence they find unclear.','Your pick-up helper walks children aged 6 to 10 from the school gate to home, 3 days a week. Over a 4-week trial that is 3 x 4 = 12 visits per child, and the sheet says clearly that you do not give medicine or take children by car.','Write a one-page offer sheet and test it with two parents, recording what they found unclear and what you changed.','State age range, times, inclusions, exclusions, safety steps and price on the sheet, and include no claim you cannot prove.','Why state what you do not do?','Clear limits protect children, avoid misunderstandings and keep you inside the rules and your insurance.'),
  M('Price and costs','Evaluate',12,'Childcare costs include more than your time. Insurance, training, travel and materials all belong in the price. You will build a cost sheet and find the number of visits you need to break even.','List the cost of each visit: your time at a fair hourly rate, travel, materials and a share of insurance and training.|Add monthly fixed costs, such as insurance, first-aid training and background checks, with the currency on each line.|Margin per visit equals price minus cost per visit, and break-even visits equal fixed costs divided by margin.|Compare your price with similar local services and explain any difference.|Ask your mentor to check the sheet before you give any price to a family.','Cost per visit is EUR 6.00 for time, 1.50 for travel, 1.50 for an insurance share and 2.00 for materials, which is EUR 11.00. At a price of EUR 15 the margin is 15 - 11 = EUR 4, and with EUR 120 fixed costs a month you need 120 / 4 = 30 visits to break even.','Build a cost sheet with cost per visit, price, margin and break-even visits, and have it checked by your mentor.','Give every line a currency, calculate the break-even correctly, and have the mentor sign the sheet.','Why include insurance and training in the cost?','They are real costs of running the service safely, and leaving them out makes the price too low to survive a loss.'),
  M('Run 30 days','Apply',34,'A trial must be small, supervised and fully recorded. You keep a daily log and report any incident the same day. A weekly review with your adviser keeps safety at the centre.','Start with a small number of families, such as 3 to 6, so that you can give each child proper attention.|Write a daily log with the date, the children present, what happened and any problem or near miss.|Report any accident, injury or serious concern to the parent and your adviser on the same day.|Hold a 15-minute weekly review with your adviser and write down one change after each.|Check the safety plan each week and change it if something new appears.','In week 2 the log shows two late pick-ups at the school gate. You add a reminder text sent 15 minutes before pick-up, and the following week there are 0 late pick-ups.','Run the 30-day trial with a daily log, same-day incident reports and four weekly reviews with your adviser.','Keep the log with no missing days, file a same-day report for each incident, and record one change in each weekly review.','What do you do when an incident happens?','Tell the parent and your adviser the same day, record it in the log and review the safety plan.'),
  M('Read feedback','Analyse',12,'Parents will tell you the truth if you ask short, clear questions. Their answers show what to keep and what to change. You summarise the results with simple percentages and choose two changes.','Send a short survey of five or six questions, covering trust, price, convenience and one open question.|Use a scale of 1 to 5 for the rating questions and add one free-text question such as what would you change.|Calculate the share of parents scoring 4 or 5 on each question.|Compare each result to the goal you set before the trial, for example 80 percent trust.|Pick two changes only, and write how you will check each one worked.','Eight of 10 parents say they trust the service, which is 8 / 10 = 80%, and 3 of 10 say price is high, which is 30%. You choose two changes: a lower price for three visits a week and a weekly photo-free message update.','Run a short survey with all families, summarise it in a table with percentages, and choose two changes.','Show percentages for trust, price and convenience in the table, compare them to your goals, and add a check for each of the two changes.','What three things should the survey ask about?','Trust, price and convenience, plus one open question for ideas and concerns.'),
  M('Plan next steps','Evaluate',16,'After the trial you decide whether to keep, change or stop. The decision rests on safety record, trust and cost. You write a short plan for the next 90 days with numbers.','Review the safety log first, and stop or pause if there was any serious incident that you cannot control.|Compare actual costs and visits with your plan and calculate the margin you really made.|List the top risks for growth, such as not enough trained helpers, and one step to reduce each.|Set a limit on growth, for example no more than 6 families until a second trained helper is available.|Plan dates for the next review with your adviser and the next parent survey.','Growing to 6 families with 3 visits a week for 4 weeks is 6 x 3 x 4 = 72 visits. At a margin of EUR 4 per visit that earns 72 x 4 = EUR 288, minus EUR 120 fixed costs, leaving EUR 168.','Present a 90-day plan with a safety review, a cost check, growth limit and dates for the next review.','Include the safety record, a margin calculation, a growth limit and dated review points in the plan.','What should decide whether you grow?',"The safety record, parents' trust and whether costs are covered with a margin.")],
 safety=SAFE)
S('NL-07','Trading and Small Import-Export','Plan and run a small, legal trading deal from sourcing to delivery, with every cost and rule counted.',
 'Plan one small trade from supplier to buyer and complete a trial order with a full cost record.','About 130 hours over 3 to 5 months. Computer, free spreadsheet, two supplier quotes, a friendly buyer, a trade adviser, mentor and peer circle.',
 'Two quotes compared; landed-cost sheet; rule check; order record; margin calculated; at least 70% on the check.',
 [('Understand', 'Explain the trade chain and who does what.'), ('Apply', 'Compare suppliers and quotes.'), ('Analyse', 'Calculate landed cost and margin.'), ('Create', 'Create a one-page trade plan.')],
 ['Trading assistant', 'Import coordinator', 'Procurement assistant', 'Small trader'],
 [M('The trade chain','Understand',8,'Goods pass through several hands before they reach a buyer, and each step adds cost and risk. Drawing the chain shows where your business fits and where money is made or lost. You will map one product from maker to buyer.','Name every step: maker, packer, trader, carrier, customs, importer, shop and final buyer, and write who pays whom at each step.|Write the cost or price added at each step so that you can see the total from maker to buyer.|Mark the points of risk, such as delay at the border, damage in transport, late payment and changes in currency rates.|Decide which step you will do yourself and which you will buy from others.|A common mistake is to forget small steps such as storage, handling and local transport, which can add several percent to the cost.','A spice chain adds EUR 2.00 per kg at the farm, +0.60 packing, +0.40 shipping, +0.50 import costs, which is EUR 3.50 by the time it reaches the importer. The shop adds 1.50 and sells for EUR 5.00 per kg.','Draw a chain for one product with at least five steps, the cost added at each step and the three biggest risks.','Give every step a cost, match the total to the final price and name three risks with a way to reduce each.','Why map the whole chain before you buy?','It shows every cost and risk so you can price correctly and avoid surprises such as delay or hidden fees.'),
  M('Find and compare suppliers','Apply',14,'The cheapest quote is not always the best deal. You compare price, quality, delivery time and payment terms side by side. Samples help you check quality before you promise anything to a buyer.',"Collect at least two written quotes for the same product, quantity and delivery terms, so they can be compared fairly.|Build a table with unit price, minimum order, lead time, payment terms and quality notes for each supplier.|Ask for samples, test them yourself and ask a trusted adviser or the buyer to look at them.|Check the supplier's papers, for example business registration and product certificates, and ask for references.|Choose by total value, not by price alone, and write the reason in one sentence.",'Quote A is EUR 4 per unit with 4 weeks lead time and quote B is EUR 5 with 1 week. For 500 units A costs 500 x 4 = EUR 2000 and B costs 500 x 5 = EUR 2500, so A is EUR 500 cheaper but 3 weeks slower.','Compare two real or realistic quotes in a table and write a one-sentence supplier choice with reasons.','Cover price, minimum order, lead time, terms and quality in the table, and explain the choice by numbers.','What do you compare besides price and why ask for samples?','Compare quality, lead time, minimum order and payment terms. Samples let you check quality before you promise goods to a buyer.'),
  M('Rules and papers','Apply',16,'Every trade is bound by rules about what can be imported or exported, what tax applies and what papers must travel with the goods. Breaking the rules can mean seized goods and fines. You check the rules before you buy anything.','Check with your local authority which goods are restricted, need a permit or need a health or safety certificate, and whether the goods come from inside or outside the EU single market, since customs duties usually apply only to goods from outside it.|Find out which duties and taxes apply, and ask the authority how they are calculated, for example as a percentage of goods value plus freight.|Keep copies of every invoice, packing list, transport paper and payment record in an organised folder.|Never trade goods that are banned or that you cannot prove are legal, and walk away from any deal that asks you to hide something.|Write a rules checklist with the date you checked each item and the person or office that answered.','A food import from outside the EU may need a health certificate from the exporting country. You confirm with your local authority and record the date, the answer and the paper you need, before you place any order.','Write a rules checklist for your product with each rule, the source you asked and the date.','Cover restrictions, duties and papers in the checklist, and take the answers from the competent authority.','Why check the rules before ordering?','Goods that break the rules can be seized or returned, and you lose the money you paid for them.'),
  M('Landed cost','Evaluate',16,'Landed cost is the full cost of getting goods to your door, ready to sell. It includes product, freight, duties, insurance and handling. Knowing it per unit stops you from pricing below your real cost.','Add up the product cost, freight, duties and taxes (ask the tax office whether import sales tax can be reclaimed), insurance, port or handling fees and local transport, each with a currency.|Divide the total by the number of saleable units, not by the number you ordered, because damaged or lost units add cost.|Add a risk allowance, for example 3 percent, for currency changes and small surprises.|Use one currency throughout and write the exchange rate and date if you convert.|Keep the sheet updated with real invoices as they arrive.','For 1000 units: product 4000 + freight 600 + duties 400 + insurance 100 = EUR 5100, so landed cost is 5100 / 1000 = EUR 5.10 per unit. With a 3% allowance, 5.10 x 1.03 = EUR 5.25.','Build a landed-cost sheet for your product with each cost line, cost per unit and a risk allowance.','Give each line a currency, calculate per unit correctly and show the allowance separately.','What is the landed cost per unit for EUR 5100 total and 1000 units?','5100 / 1000 = EUR 5.10 per unit, before any risk allowance.'),
  M('Price and margin','Evaluate',12,'Your price must cover landed cost, selling costs and a profit, even when something goes wrong. Margin is measured against the selling price, while markup is measured against cost. You will calculate both and test a price with a buyer.','Margin equals price minus cost, divided by price, and shows the share of each sale that is profit.|Markup equals price minus cost, divided by cost, and is always bigger than margin for the same numbers, so do not mix them.|Subtract selling costs, such as marketplace fees, packaging and delivery, before you call it profit.|Test your price with at least two buyers and note what they say, since a price nobody questions is probably too low.|Allow for returns or damaged goods, for example 2 percent, in your margin.','With landed cost EUR 5.10 and a price of EUR 7.00, profit per unit is 7.00 - 5.10 = EUR 1.90. Margin is 1.90 / 7 = 27%, and markup is 1.90 / 5.10 = 37%.','Calculate margin and markup for your product two ways and record what two buyers said about the price.','Check that the two calculation routes agree, include selling costs and write down the buyer comments.','What is the margin at a price of EUR 7 and a cost of EUR 5.10?','(7 - 5.10) / 7 = 0.27, so the margin is 27%.'),
  M('Pay and protect','Apply',14,'Cash is the biggest risk in trade, because you often pay before goods arrive. Written payment terms and sensible protection reduce that risk. You will write terms that share the risk fairly.',"Agree payment terms in writing, including the amount, the due dates and the method such as bank transfer.|Split the payment, for example 30 percent on order and 70 percent on delivery or on inspection.|Check the supplier's bank details by a second route, such as a phone call to a known number, before the first payment.|Consider insurance for goods in transit and ask your adviser whether it fits your order.|Keep every payment receipt and match it to an invoice.",'On an order worth EUR 2000, a 30% deposit is 0.30 x 2000 = EUR 600 and the remaining 70% is EUR 1400 due on delivery. If the goods never arrive you have lost EUR 600 and not the full EUR 2000.',"Write payment terms for your trial order with amounts, dates, method and a check of the supplier's bank details.",'Put the terms in writing, check that both parts add up to the full price and check the bank details by a second route.','Why split the payment into two parts?','It shares the risk between buyer and seller, so neither side carries the full loss if something goes wrong.'),
  M('Run a trial order','Apply',40,'A small first order tests your whole plan at low cost. You track dates, costs and problems from order to delivery. What you learn here is worth more than the profit.','Place a small order, for example 100 units, that you can afford to lose.|Write down the date of every step: order, payment, dispatch, arrival, inspection and delivery to the buyer.|Record every actual cost with its invoice and compare it to the landed-cost sheet.|Inspect the goods on arrival, count them, photograph damage and tell the supplier within the agreed time.|Keep a problem list and note what you would do differently next time.','Your 100-unit order has a planned cost of 100 x 5.10 = EUR 510. It arrives 3 days late, and two units are damaged, so you can sell 98 units and record both facts with photos.','Run a trial order and keep a dated record of costs, dates, inspection results and problems.','Record every step with dates and costs, and give all problems a note and a next action.','Why start with a small order?','Problems appear at low cost, so you can fix your process before you risk much money.'),
  M('Review','Evaluate',10,'A review compares plan with actual and turns the trial into decisions. You list lessons and set a next order. Honest numbers matter more than a good story.','Compare planned and actual cost, time and quality in one table, with the difference in currency and percent.|Find the biggest gap and ask why five times to reach the root cause.|Write three lessons in plain words, each with a number that proves it.|Decide whether to repeat, change supplier, change price or stop, and write the reason.|Plan the next order with a quantity, a date and a budget.','Planned cost was EUR 510 and actual cost was EUR 540.60, so the difference is 540.60 - 510 = EUR 30.60. That is 30.60 / 510 = 6% above plan, mostly from extra handling.','Present a plan-versus-actual table, three lessons and a decision for the next order.','Show differences in currency and percent in the table, and cite a number for each lesson.','What do you compare after the trial order?','Compare plan against actual for cost, time and quality, and find the reason for the largest gap.')],
 safety=SAFE)
S('NL-08','Industrial Activity: Light Manufacturing','Plan, run and measure a small safe production batch, from drawing to checked product.',
 'Plan and run one small production batch with a safety plan, quality checks and a cost record.','About 150 hours over 3 to 6 months. Access to a supervised workshop, basic tools, free spreadsheet, a qualified supervisor, mentor and peer circle.',
 'Safety plan approved; batch records; quality results; cost sheet; improvement plan; at least 70% on the check.',
 [('Understand', 'Explain flow from material to checked product.'), ('Apply', 'Run a small batch safely.'), ('Analyse', 'Analyse defects and time.'), ('Create', 'Design a better layout or step.')],
 ['Production operator', 'Quality checker', 'Workshop assistant', 'Lean support assistant'],
 [M('Flow of work','Understand',10,'Every product follows a path from material to finished item. Waiting and carrying often take more time than working. In this module you draw that path and find the slowest step.','Draw each step from material in to product out, using boxes for work and arrows for movement.|Time each step with a clock on three different parts and use the average.|Mark steps that add no value, such as waiting, carrying, searching for tools and rework.|Name the slowest step, since the slowest step limits how many parts you can make in a day.|A common mistake is to speed up a fast step, which only builds a pile of parts before the slow step.','A part takes 120 minutes from start to finish, of which only 45 minutes is real work and 75 minutes is waiting and carrying. Value-adding time is 45 / 120 = 37.5%, and cutting a 40-minute wait for a tool is the best first change.','Draw a flow map of your batch process with times for each step, and mark waiting and carrying steps.','Time all steps, mark non-value steps and name the slowest step with its time.','What is a waiting step and why does the slowest step matter?','A waiting step is time when no work is done on the part. The slowest step sets the pace of the whole line.'),
  M('Safety first','Apply',16,'No batch starts without a safety plan that a qualified supervisor has signed. Safety rules differ by place, so you check with your local authority. Habits such as protection and tidy work areas prevent most injuries.','List every hazard in the batch, such as moving parts, sharp edges, dust, noise, heat, lifting and electricity.|Write a control for each hazard, such as a guard, a lock-out step, ear protection or a lifting limit.|Wear the protection the plan requires, tie back hair and loose clothing, and never wear jewellery near machines.|Learn where the emergency stop, first-aid kit and exits are before you begin.|Do not start work until a qualified supervisor has read and signed the plan, and check with your local authority for rules that apply.','Your plan lists 6 hazards for a saw station and each has a control, for example a fixed guard, eye protection and a rule to switch off before clearing offcuts. The supervisor signs and dates the plan before the first cut.',"Write a safety plan with hazards, controls and protection for your batch and obtain a supervisor's signature.",'Give each hazard a control, mark emergency points and sign and date the plan.','Who must sign off the safety plan and when?','A qualified supervisor signs it before any work on the batch starts.'),
  M('Standards and drawings','Apply',16,'Everyone must make the same part the same way. A drawing shows size and tolerance, and a gauge or tool checks it. You learn to read a simple drawing and measure with care.','Read the title, scale, units and each dimension on the drawing before you cut any material.|Tolerance is the allowed difference from the target size, written as plus or minus a number.|Choose the right tool for the tolerance, such as a steel rule for 1 mm and a calliper for 0.1 mm.|Measure at the same point on every part and write the result in a table.|Mark parts outside tolerance and set them aside, never mix them with good parts.','The drawing says 100 mm plus or minus 0.5, so a good part measures between 99.5 and 100.5 mm. A part measuring 100.3 mm passes, and a part measuring 100.7 mm fails.','Measure five parts against a drawing and record each size, the allowed range and pass or fail.','Put all five measurements in a table with the range, judge each pass or fail correctly and set failed parts aside.','What is tolerance and what range does 100 mm plus or minus 0.5 allow?','Tolerance is the allowed difference from the target size. The range is 99.5 mm to 100.5 mm.'),
  M('Plan the batch','Create',16,'A good plan lists materials, time and people before you start. It also includes an allowance for waste. You will build a bill of materials and a time plan for 50 parts.','List every material with quantity per part and for the whole batch, with units such as kilograms or metres.|Add a scrap allowance, usually 5 to 10 percent, because cutting and testing always lose some material.|Time each step per part and multiply by the batch size to find total hours.|Check stock and order missing material, noting the lead time so that you do not stop mid-batch.|Write who does each step and what tools they need.','Fifty parts need 12 kg of material, and a 10% allowance adds 1.2 kg, so you order 13.2 kg. Cutting 4 minutes, drilling 3 and sanding 2 gives 9 minutes per part, and 50 x 9 = 450 minutes, which is 7.5 hours.','Write a batch plan with a bill of materials, a scrap allowance, times per step and a list of tools and people.','Include scrap in quantities, calculate total time correctly and check stock.','Why add a scrap allowance?','Some material is always lost in cutting, setting up and testing, so ordering exactly the needed amount leaves you short.'),
  M('Run the batch','Apply',40,'Running a batch is where the plan meets reality. You log time and defects as you go and stop when the pattern changes. Tidy work keeps both quality and safety high.','Log the start and end time for each step and the number of parts finished every hour.|Check the first part fully before running the rest, and check every fifth part afterwards.|Write each defect with its type and the time it happened.|Stop and investigate if two defects of the same type happen close together, instead of continuing and wasting material.|Clean the area at the end of every shift and return tools to their place.','In a batch of 50 parts, 4 are defective, so the defect rate is 4 / 50 = 8%. Two defects of the same type appear in parts 27 and 28, so you stop and find that the blade is worn. Parts 29 and 30, already cut, are also defective. You replace the blade and the next 20 parts (31 to 50) have no defects.','Run the batch with a time log, a defect log and a record of each stop and what you changed.','Log every step, count defects by type and give each stop a cause and action.','When should you stop a batch?','Stop when defects of the same type begin to repeat, then find and fix the cause before you continue.'),
  M('Quality and defects','Analyse',20,'Quality work finds causes instead of blame. You count defects by type, find the biggest group and trace it to the root. Then you fix the cause at its source.','Count defects by type in a table and calculate each type as a percentage of all defects.|Sort the table from largest to smallest and work on the largest first.|Ask why five times: keep asking why the problem happened until you reach a cause you can change.|Fix the cause, for example replace a tool or change a method, rather than only fixing the part.|Check again after the fix and record whether the defect count fell.','Of 10 defects, 6 are burrs, 3 are wrong size and 1 is a scratch, so burrs are 6 / 10 = 60%. Asking why five times leads from burrs to a blunt blade to no sharpening schedule, so you create a schedule.','Chart the defects by type, find the root cause of the biggest group and write the corrective action.','Show counts and percentages on the chart, reach the root cause by repeated why questions and give an action an owner.','What does ask why five times mean?','It means asking why again and again until you reach a root cause you can fix, instead of stopping at the first symptom.'),
  M('Cost per part','Evaluate',12,'Cost per part decides your price and your profit. It includes material, labour, energy and waste. You count only good parts, because bad parts add cost without value.','List material cost with the scrap included, labour at a fair hourly rate, energy and tool wear.|Add up the total batch cost and divide it by the number of good parts, not by the number started.|Compare your cost per part with a price quote for the same item to see if you are competitive.|Find which line is biggest and think about how to reduce it.|Put a currency on every line and ask your mentor to check the sheet.','Material is 13.2 kg x EUR 4 = EUR 52.80, labour is 7.5 hours x EUR 15 = EUR 112.50, and energy is EUR 6.00, so the total is EUR 171.30. With 46 good parts, cost per part is 171.30 / 46 = EUR 3.72.','Build a cost sheet for the batch and calculate cost per good part.','Give all lines a currency, get the total correct, divide using good parts and have the mentor check it.','Why divide by good parts and not by parts started?','Defective parts cost money but cannot be sold, so their cost must be spread over the good parts.'),
  M('Improve','Evaluate',20,'Improvement means one small change that you measure. You choose the slowest or most wasteful step, trial a change and compare the numbers. Changing one thing at a time shows what really worked.','Pick the step that limits output or causes the most defects, using your flow map and defect chart.|Write the change you will trial, the number you expect and how you will measure it.|Change one thing only, such as a jig, a tool position or a work order, and run a small test.|Measure the same number before and after, and calculate the change in percent.|If it works, write it into the standard method, and if not, record why and try another idea.','Setup time was 20 minutes and a simple jig cut it to 14 minutes, a saving of 6 minutes. That is 6 / 20 = 30% less, and over 10 setups a week it saves 60 minutes.','Present one improvement with the problem, the change, and numbers before and after.','Measure the numbers before and after the same way, calculate the percent change correctly and change only one thing.','Why change only one thing at a time?','So you know which change caused the result, and you can repeat it with confidence.')],
 safety=SAFE)
S('NL-09','Construction Industry Skills','Plan a small, safe building task on paper and on site: measure, estimate, schedule, check quality and record.',
 'Plan and complete one small supervised construction task with a safety plan, material list, schedule and quality record.','About 150 hours over 3 to 6 months. Supervised site or workshop, tape and level, free spreadsheet, qualified supervisor, mentor and peer circle.',
 'Safety plan signed; measurements correct; material list and cost; schedule; quality record; at least 70% on the check.',
 [('Understand', 'Explain site roles and safety duties.'), ('Apply', 'Measure and set out a small task.'), ('Analyse', 'Analyse cost and delay.'), ('Create', 'Create a schedule and quality plan.')],
 ['Site assistant', 'Estimator assistant', 'Trade apprentice', 'Quantity helper'],
 [M('Site roles and safety','Understand',12,'A building site works safely only when everyone knows who decides what. Safety duties apply to every person, from the client to the newest helper. In this module you learn the roles and the habits that prevent accidents.','Name the main roles: client, designer, main contractor, supervisor and each trade, and write what each one decides.|Hold a short safety talk of about 10 minutes at the start of each day to cover the task, hazards and emergency points.|Check with your local authority which permits, training and protection are required for your task, for example for work at height or in a trench.|Report hazards at once, and know that anyone on site, and always the supervisor, can stop unsafe work.|Wear the required protection, usually a helmet, boots, gloves and eye protection, and keep the site tidy.','A trench needs support before anyone goes in, and the supervisor stops work until it is in place. The delay is 2 hours, which costs 2 hours x EUR 15 x 3 workers = EUR 90 in wages but avoids the risk of a collapse.','Write a one-page roles and safety sheet with each role, daily safety talk topics, protection needed and emergency points.','Name all roles with decisions, give hazards controls, and ask a supervisor to check the sheet.','Who can stop unsafe work on a site?','Anyone on site can raise a stop, and the supervisor has the duty to stop work until it is safe.'),
  M('Read plans','Understand',14,'Plans show where things go and how big they are. You must read scale, symbols and dimensions before you measure or cut anything. In this module you practise reading a simple plan and converting scale to real size.','Find the scale in the title block, for example 1:50, meaning 1 unit on paper equals 50 units on site.|To find real length, multiply the measured length on paper by the scale number, then convert units.|Learn the common symbols for walls, doors, windows and levels, and check the legend on the drawing.|Compare the plan with the section and detail drawings so that heights and depths match.|Check each dimension twice and write down any that do not add up, then ask the designer.','At 1:50, a line that measures 40 mm on paper is 40 x 50 = 2000 mm, which is 2 m. A wall of 6.5 m would be 6500 / 50 = 130 mm long on the drawing.','Read a simple plan and list ten dimensions with their real size, scale used and any mismatch you found.','Calculate all ten real sizes correctly, show units and record any mismatch.','At a scale of 1:50, how long is 40 mm on paper?','40 x 50 = 2000 mm, which is 2 m.'),
  M('Measure and set out','Apply',20,'Accurate setting out saves rework and money. You mark lines on the ground with a tape and check them for square and level. Small errors at the start grow into big errors later.','Use a steel tape pulled straight and read it from the same end each time, and write the number straight away.|Check a rectangle is square by measuring both diagonals, which are equal if the corners are right angles.|Use the 3-4-5 rule for a quick right angle: sides of 3 m and 4 m give a diagonal of 5 m.|Check levels with a spirit level or water level and mark the level on pegs.|Record every reading, and re-measure if two readings differ by more than 5 mm.','For a slab of 4 m by 3 m, both diagonals should measure 5 m, because 3 squared plus 4 squared is 25 and the square root is 5. If one diagonal reads 5.02 m and the other 4.98 m, the difference is 4 cm, which means the frame is not square and needs adjusting.','Set out a small rectangle, check the diagonals and level, and record readings in a table.','Measure sizes within 5 mm, check that diagonals agree and record every reading.','How do equal diagonals show a rectangle is square?','In a rectangle with right-angle corners both diagonals have the same length, so unequal diagonals show the frame is out of square.'),
  M('Quantities and cost','Evaluate',20,'Counting before buying prevents shortages and waste. You work out volumes and areas, add waste and price each item. A clear bill helps the client understand the cost.','Volume equals length times width times depth, all in metres, and the result is in cubic metres.|Area equals length times width in square metres, used for items such as boards or paint.|Add 5 to 10 percent for waste depending on the material, and round up to the unit you buy.|Multiply quantity by price per unit and add the lines to get a total, with the currency on every line.|Ask a supplier for a written price and note how long it is valid.','A slab of 4 x 3 x 0.1 m has a volume of 1.2 m3. Adding 7% waste gives 1.2 x 1.07 = 1.284 m3, rounded up to 1.3 m3 to order, and at EUR 110 per m3 the cost is 1.3 x 110 = EUR 143.00.','Build a bill of quantities for your task with units, waste, price and total cost.','Calculate volumes and areas correctly, show waste, put currency on every line and add up the total.','What is the volume of a slab 4 m by 3 m by 0.1 m?','4 x 3 x 0.1 = 1.2 cubic metres, before any waste allowance.'),
  M('Schedule','Create',16,'A schedule puts the tasks in order and shows how long each one takes. Some tasks must wait for others, such as concrete that needs time to cure. A buffer protects the plan from weather and late deliveries.',"List every task with its length in days, such as dig, formwork, reinforcement, pour and cure.|Mark which tasks depend on others, and which can run at the same time.|Include waiting periods, for example concrete usually needs several days before it can carry load, and follow the supplier's instructions.|Add a buffer of about 15 to 20 percent for weather and delivery delays.|Draw a simple bar chart with a day number on each bar and review it with your supervisor.",'Dig 1 day, formwork 1, reinforcement 1, pour 1 and cure 7 days make 11 days. A 20% buffer is 11 x 0.20 = 2.2, rounded to 2 days, so the plan runs for 13 days.','Build a bar chart schedule with task durations, dependencies and a buffer.','Order the work logically, show waiting periods and include a buffer in the total.','Why add a buffer to the schedule?','Weather, late deliveries and rework often cause delays, and a buffer keeps the end date realistic.'),
  M('Do the task','Apply',40,'Doing the task safely and recording each stage proves the quality of your work. Photos and a daily log protect you and the client. Problems fixed at once cost less than problems found later.','Hold the daily safety talk, check protection and tools, and walk the area for hazards before work starts.|Follow the plan and the schedule, and write in the log what was done, by whom and how long it took.|Take photos of each stage, especially work that will be covered such as reinforcement and pipes.|Measure and check each stage against the plan before you move to the next.|Fix errors straight away and record what was wrong and what you did.','Before the pour you take 4 photos of the reinforcement with a tape in view, and you measure the cover as 40 mm against a plan value of 40 mm. The photos prove the steel position after it is covered by concrete.','Complete the task under supervision with a daily log, stage photos and measured checks.','Keep the log free of missing days, photograph each hidden stage, and write checks with measured numbers.','Why photograph each stage of the work?','Photos prove what is hidden later and help settle questions about quality.'),
  M('Quality and defects','Analyse',16,'Quality checks find faults early, when they are cheap to fix. You use a checklist for each stage and measure against tolerance. A snag list collects faults to fix before handover.','Write a short checklist for each stage, with the item, the target and the tolerance.|Measure with the right tool, for example a straightedge and ruler for surface level over 3 m, and record the number.|Compare each reading to the tolerance and mark pass or fail.|List each fault on a snag list with its place, size, cause and who will fix it.|Re-check each fix and close the item only when it passes.','The tolerance for surface flatness is 5 mm under a 3 m straightedge, and you measure a gap of 8 mm. That fails by 3 mm, so you grind the high spot and re-test, and the second reading is 3 mm, which passes.','Run a quality check on your task with a checklist, measurements and a snag list with closed items.','Show target, reading and pass or fail for each item, and fix and re-test all snags.','What is a snag list?','It is a list of faults that must be fixed before handover, with place, cause and who fixes each one.'),
  M('Handover and review','Evaluate',12,'A clean handover with full records builds trust. You give the client the documents, photos and care notes. A short review captures lessons for the next task.','Clear and clean the site, remove waste properly and make sure no hazards are left.|Prepare a handover pack with drawings, photos, material records, test results and care instructions.|Walk through the work with the client or supervisor and show the snag list as closed.|Compare planned and actual time and cost, and write the difference in days and currency.|Write three lessons and one change you will make next time.','Planned time was 13 days and actual time was 14 days, so you were 1 day late, which is 1 / 13 = 8% over. Planned cost was EUR 143 for concrete and actual EUR 150, so the difference is EUR 7.','Prepare a handover pack and present a review that compares plan and actual with three lessons.','Assemble a pack containing drawings, photos, test results and care notes, and show differences in days and currency in the review.','What goes in a handover pack?','Drawings, photos of each stage, material and test records, and care instructions.')],
 safety='Learning only. Work only under a qualified supervisor with the required site safety training and protection.')
S('NL-10','Agriculture and Fishing: Sustainable Practice','Plan and run one small, sustainable growing or catch-handling project and record yields, costs and care for land or water.',
 'Plan and run one small sustainable production or handling project for 60 days with a care plan, yield record and cost sheet.','About 140 hours over 3 to 6 months. Small plot, tank or partner holding, free spreadsheet, adviser, mentor and peer circle.',
 'Site plan; care plan; 60-day log of inputs and yield; cost per kilo; lessons; at least 70% on the check.',
 [('Understand', 'Explain how soil, water and weather shape a plan.'), ('Apply', 'Run a small plot or handling process.'), ('Analyse', 'Analyse yield and cost.'), ('Create', 'Create a care plan for land or water.')],
 ['Farm assistant', 'Greenhouse helper', 'Fish-handling assistant', 'Agribusiness assistant'],
 [M('Read the site','Understand',10,'Soil, water, light and wind decide what can grow or live on a site. Reading the site before you plan saves money and failed crops. You make a simple map and test the basics.','Test soil with a simple kit for acidity (pH) and note texture, for example sandy, loamy or clay, by feeling a moist sample.|For water, test or ask a local adviser about temperature, oxygen and clarity, and record the date and place of each test.|Map the sun hours, wind direction, slope and places where water collects.|Check local rules about land use, water use and wildlife, and check with your local authority before you start.|Write a short site note saying which crops or species fit and which do not.','Your 10 m2 plot has a soil pH of 6.5, which suits salad leaves, and the north corner stays wet after rain. You choose raised beds for that corner and keep the dry part for direct sowing.','Build a site map with tests, sun and wind notes, wet and dry areas and a short list of suitable crops.','Date the map and tests, check the rules, and link each choice to a result in the site note.','Why test the site before planting?','To match the crop or species to the soil or water, so you do not waste seed, time and money.'),
  M('Choose and plan','Create',14,'Small and well-chosen beats big and wild. You pick one crop or species, write a calendar and plan your inputs. A clear plan makes your records easy to compare later.','Pick one crop or species that fits your site, season and skills, and avoid starting with several at once.|Write a calendar with sowing, care, harvest and sale dates and the number of days between them.|Plan inputs such as seed, compost, water and tools, with quantities and costs.|Set a target yield and a target cost per kilogram before you start, so that you can judge results later.|Ask an adviser to look at the plan and note any change.','Salad leaves need about 28 days from sowing to harvest, so you sow a new strip every 14 days. Over 8 weeks that gives 4 sowings, and you set a target of 20 kg from 10 m2.','Write a plan with a calendar, input list with quantities and costs, and targets for yield and cost.','Include dates, quantities, targets and an adviser comment in the plan.','Why start with one crop or species?','It is easier to learn, record and compare, and mistakes cost less.'),
  M('Inputs and care','Apply',20,'Water, feed and tools are your inputs, and using them wisely saves money and protects the environment. You measure what you use so you can improve it. Clean tools and tanks prevent disease.','Measure water by volume each time, for example with a marked can or a meter, and write it in the log.|Use compost or other organic feed where possible, and record the weight or volume used.|Water at cool times of day, early morning or evening, to reduce loss to the sun.|Clean tools, tanks and containers after use, and keep a note of the date.|Compare inputs against your plan each week and adjust when use is too high.','At 20 L per watering, 3 times a week for 8 weeks, you use 20 x 3 x 8 = 480 L. A drip line cuts this by 40%, so you use 480 x 0.60 = 288 L, saving 192 L.','Keep a weekly input log with water, feed and cleaning, and compare it to your plan.','Show amounts and units for every week in the log, and explain differences from the plan.','Why measure inputs?','You cannot improve what you do not measure, and records show waste and savings.'),
  M('Pests, disease and welfare','Apply',16,'Prevention is the first answer to pests, disease and poor welfare. Regular checks find problems early and help you choose the least harmful control. Animals and fish need clear welfare care under local rules.','Check a sample of 20 plants or animals weekly and note signs of damage, disease or stress.|Use prevention first, such as netting, crop rotation, clean water and good spacing.|If control is needed, choose the least harmful method first, and check with your local authority and adviser before using any treatment.|For animals or fish, provide space, clean water, suitable feed and shelter, and remove stressed or sick ones as advised.|Record every action and the result a week later.','In a weekly check of 20 plants, 3 have bird damage, which is 3 / 20 = 15%. Your action level is 10%, so you add netting and the next check shows 1 of 20, which is 5%.','Complete weekly checks on a sample, record the results as percentages and write one prevention action.','Date the checks, calculate percentages correctly and record a result for each action.','What is the first step against pests?','Prevention and regular checks, before any treatment.'),
  M('Run the 60 days','Apply',40,'Running the project means caring every day and recording every week. You weigh harvest and note weather. These records are the evidence for everything you decide afterwards.','Follow your calendar and write down any change and the reason.|Record daily or weekly water, feed, labour hours and weather, using the same units each time.|Weigh each harvest on the same scale and write the date, weight and quality.|Calculate yield per square metre or per tank volume at each harvest.|Take photos at the start, middle and end to show progress.','A harvest of 18 kg from a 10 m2 plot gives a yield of 18 / 10 = 1.8 kg per m2. If you plan 20 kg, you reached 18 / 20 = 90% of your target.','Run the 60 days with a log of inputs, weather and harvest weights, and calculate yield.','Keep the log free of long gaps, give weights dates and units, and calculate yield correctly.','What is the yield per m2 for 18 kg from 10 m2?','18 / 10 = 1.8 kg per m2.'),
  M('Cost per kilo','Evaluate',14,'Cost per kilo shows whether the project can pay for itself. You add all costs and divide by the weight sold or used. Comparing with a market price shows whether your result is competitive.','List all costs, such as seed, water, tools, packaging and labour hours at a fair rate, with currency on each line.|Add the lines to get the total cost.|Divide total cost by the kilograms harvested to get cost per kilo.|Compare to the local market price per kilo and note the difference.|Find the largest cost line and write one idea to reduce it.','Seed EUR 6, water EUR 4, tools share EUR 8 and labour EUR 8 give a total of EUR 26. Cost per kilo is 26 / 18 = EUR 1.44, and if the market pays EUR 3 per kilo the gross margin is 3 - 1.44 = EUR 1.56 per kilo.','Build a cost sheet with all costs, cost per kilo and a comparison to the market price.','Give all lines a currency, divide correctly and show the comparison.','What is the cost per kilo for EUR 26 and 18 kg?','26 / 18 = EUR 1.44 per kilo.'),
  M('Storage and sale','Apply',12,'Fresh produce loses quality fast. Cooling, clean packing and quick sale protect value and food safety. You record how much you sell, share or lose.','Cool the harvest quickly and keep it cool, because warmth speeds spoilage.|Pack in clean containers and label them with the date and contents.|Follow local rules on selling food or fish, and check with your local authority about labelling and hygiene.|Sell or share within a few days, and record the amounts sold, given away and wasted.|Calculate waste as a percentage and think of one way to reduce it.','Of 18 kg harvested, you sell 12 kg at EUR 3, which is EUR 36, share 4 kg and lose 2 kg. Waste is 2 / 18 = 11%, and the cold box keeps leaves fresh for 5 days.','Sell or share the harvest and record the amounts sold, shared, lost and the income.','Add the amounts up to the harvest weight, calculate income correctly and show waste as a percentage.','Why cool the harvest quickly?','Cooling slows spoilage, so the produce keeps its quality and value for longer.'),
  M('Review and care plan','Evaluate',14,'A review compares plan with result and plans the next season. Good practice leaves land or water in better condition. You write a short care plan for soil or water.','Compare planned and actual yield, cost and time in one table with percentages.|Write three lessons, each with a number from your records.|Plan the next season, such as a different crop to rest the soil, for example beans after leaves.|Write a care plan for soil or water, with compost, cover crops, clean water or reduced inputs.|Set a date to review the plan and a target number to reach.','Planned yield was 20 kg and actual yield was 18 kg, which is 90% of plan. Planned cost per kilo was EUR 1.30 and actual was EUR 1.44, so cost was about 11% higher, and you plan to cut water use.','Present a review with plan against actual, three lessons and a care plan for next season.','Show percentages in the table, cite a number for each lesson and add dated actions to the care plan.','Why plan the next season and care for soil or water?','Care keeps the land or water healthy so future yields stay good, and planning avoids repeating mistakes.')],
 safety=SAFE)
